From: ProjectM TV contributors
Subject: [PATCH] Guard PowerVR framebuffer deletion on both binding targets

Adapt Chromium's ensure_previous_framebuffer_not_deleted workaround and the
read/draw deletion repair in Skia commit
02b14267cb023e7ec9fc52618bd12464ceb6afb7 (2026-10-12):
https://skia.googlesource.com/skia/+/02b14267cb023e7ec9fc52618bd12464ceb6afb7

Imagination drivers can retain a reference to the previous complete FBO. A
single bind to zero before deletion leaves the object as the previous binding.
Create one complete 1x1 renderbuffer-backed guard per rendering thread/context;
bind it then zero on both targets before deleting an FBO, preserving surviving
caller read/draw bindings afterwards. Retain the guard until context destruction
so its own deletion cannot recreate the hazard. Repeated instances on the same
context reuse it; the explicit context-loss hook forgets names without GL calls
before a replacement instance initializes. The existing texture-pool context-loss
hook also forgets the guard for compatibility with existing C API hosts. Other
vendors keep the raw GL path.

Cover engine, temporary clear/copy/probe and host-owned FBO retirement. This is
a compatibility candidate for preset-switch crashes on PowerVR GE9215; host
GL invariant/output tests do not establish that the affected Fire TV is fixed.

---
diff --git a/src/api/include/projectM-4/render_opengl.h b/src/api/include/projectM-4/render_opengl.h
index 67f123478..e007ec870 100644
--- a/src/api/include/projectM-4/render_opengl.h
+++ b/src/api/include/projectM-4/render_opengl.h
@@ -167,10 +167,25 @@ PROJECTM_EXPORT void projectm_opengl_set_line_antialiasing(projectm_handle insta
 PROJECTM_EXPORT void projectm_opengl_set_texture_pool_limit(size_t max_bytes);
 
 /**
- * @brief Drops the calling thread's pooled textures without deleting them, after their context was lost.
+ * @brief Drops the calling thread's pooled textures and framebuffer deletion guard after context loss.
+ * No GL calls are issued. Existing hosts using this context-loss hook also forget guard names.
+ * Call before retiring stale instances on a replacement context on the owning rendering thread.
  */
 PROJECTM_EXPORT void projectm_opengl_forget_texture_pool();
 
+/** Forget the calling thread's PowerVR deletion guard after context loss, without GL calls.
+ * Call before retiring a stale instance on a replacement context. The next instance initializes
+ * the replacement guard; repeated instances on the same context reuse the existing guard.
+ */
+PROJECTM_EXPORT void projectm_opengl_forget_framebuffer_guard();
+
+/** Delete host-owned FBOs with the engine's PowerVR compatibility workaround.
+ * Call on the initialized engine's GL thread/context. Preserves surviving read/draw bindings;
+ * bindings named in ids become zero, as with glDeleteFramebuffers. Other vendors use GL directly.
+ * Keep each rendering thread paired with one context; forget the guard when replacing that context.
+ */
+PROJECTM_EXPORT void projectm_opengl_delete_framebuffers(int count, const unsigned int* ids);
+
 /**
  * @brief Memory held by the calling thread's texture pool, in bytes.
  */
